.TH __CHTTPCLIENT_DESTROY 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
__chttpclient_destroy \- internal destroy function
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <chttpclient.h>

void __chttpclient_destroy(chttpcli cli);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
cli is an opaque VALUE handle (a packed {slot index, generation}
pair), resolved through a library-owned slot table before the
underlying client object is ever touched. cli must be a currently
live handle (one returned by create_chttpclient/_mp or
chttp_default_client and not yet destroyed). CHTTPCLI_INVALID is a
silent no-op. Any other value (a stale handle already destroyed by
an earlier call or concurrently by another thread, a forged value,
or garbage) is a FATAL ERROR: this function calls fatal_err()
(abort()/SIGABRT) rather than risk a use-after-free or double-free.

On a live handle, waits for all in-flight requests to complete
(both a synchronous chttpclient_do/_streaming call still executing
on another thread, and any Tier 2/3 chttpclient_do_async/_streaming
or chttpclient_do_pooled/_streaming request submitted to the shared
reactor engine that has not yet completed), then closes all pooled
connections (both idle and TLS), frees the idle-connection pool,
destroys the client's TLS context, frees synchronisation
primitives, and frees the client struct.

If cli is the handle returned by chttp_default_client(), this
module's own reference to it is cleared first so a subsequent
chttp_default_client() call returns CHTTPCLI_INVALID instead of a
stale handle; see chttp_default_client(3)'s own WARNINGS section.

Do not call directly; use the chttpclient_destroy macro, which also
sets the handle to CHTTPCLI_INVALID.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpclient_destroy (3),
.BR ___chttpclient_destroy (3),
.BR chttp_default_client (3)
